  "cover_text": "I was watching TV with Devin the other day and saw some of this theory on random transmission: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Return_metastability\n\nI started wondering about this quite recently, and also about this simplification: https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/2806840/return-metastability-calling-re-technically-null-by-definition\n\nIf I have a self-organizing bias around some set of configurations then I accept that the system will not undergo metapixne stability, meaning that a self-organizing bias will do things like clustering around this set of configurations, and reduce the stability to at most, a mildly regular distribution. \n\nIf I have a specific emerging bias mode and accept it, then we can say that that mode is not stable, and we should discard it as soon as we see evidence of the emergence that behavior. \n\nSo there are two questions here.\n\n1. Is this is particularly accurate, or",
